# Line endings are load-bearing in this repository.
#
# `crates/l2-mods/rulesets/core/rules/*.toml` are *generated* documents, and
# `crates/l2-mods/tests/core.rs` compares them byte for byte against the
# renderer that produced them. The renderer emits "\n".
#
# GitHub's Windows runners default to `core.autocrlf=true`, so a fresh checkout
# there rewrote those files with CRLF and the comparison failed - green on
# Linux, red on Windows, and passing on every developer machine configured with
# `autocrlf=input`. Normalising here fixes it for every checkout on every
# platform, which a per-test workaround would not: a test that shrugged off
# line endings would also stop noticing real drift in the generated files.
#
# `eol=lf` (not just `text=auto`) is the part that matters - it pins the
# *working tree*, overriding whatever core.autocrlf the machine has.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts that people may open in tools which insist on CRLF.
# Nothing compares these byte-for-byte.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Never touch these, whatever heuristics would otherwise guess.
# The asset types are gitignored and must never be committed (CLAUDE.md rule 1);
# they are listed anyway so that a force-add cannot silently corrupt one.
*.pl8  binary
*.256  binary
*.smk  binary
*.wav  binary
*.saf  binary
*.sav  binary
*.dat  binary
*.exe  binary
*.dll  binary
*.png  binary
*.bmp  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.bundle binary
